<!-- §01 THE QUADRATIC WALL -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Quadratic Wall</div>
<h2>Why Long Context Kills Attention</h2>
<p>Standard attention is elegant: every query token attends to every key-value token. But elegance has a cost. With a sequence of length L, the attention matrix is L × L — quadratic memory and quadratic compute. At L = 128K, that's 16 billion cells per layer, per forward pass.</p>
<p>DeepSeek-V3.1 already used Multi-head Latent Attention (MLA), which compressed the KV cache with a low-rank latent vector. But MLA still computes full attention: every query must score every past token. For a live service processing hundreds of 128K-context requests simultaneously on H800 clusters, even the <em>attention kernel</em> becomes the cost bottleneck — not I/O, not FFN layers.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">attention cost vs context length — dense vs sparse</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">interactive</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="cost-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div class="cost-chart-legend">
      <span><span class="dot" style="background:#e06c75"></span>Dense MLA — O(L²)</span>
      <span><span class="dot" style="background:#8b5cf6"></span>DSA — O(L·k), k=2048</span>
      <span><span class="dot" style="background:#10b981"></span>Lightning Indexer — O(L²) but tiny constant</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The red curve is brutal: a 4× increase in context length means a 16× increase in attention FLOPs. The purple DSA curve is linear — with k=2048 fixed, the main attention cost grows at the same rate regardless of L. The green indexer curve is also O(L²) but with a constant so much smaller (fewer heads, FP8 precision) that it's negligible at any practical context length.</p>
<p>The core insight: <strong>not all tokens are equally important for any given query</strong>. A question about "Python decorators" at position 100K doesn't need to attend to the 80K tokens about unrelated topics. If we can quickly identify which tokens are relevant, we only need to attend to those.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 MLA BACKGROUND -->
<section id="s-mla" data-code="mla">
<div class="section-no">02 — MLA Background</div>
<h2>How MLA Compresses the KV Cache</h2>
<p>Before DSA, DeepSeek used Multi-head Latent Attention (MLA), introduced in DeepSeek-V2. Standard multi-head attention (MHA) caches one full key and value vector per head per position — memory that scales as H × d_k × L. For a model with many heads and long sequences, this cache becomes the bottleneck.</p>
<p>MLA's solution: compress all heads into a single low-rank <em>latent vector</em> c<sub>s</sub><sup>KV</sup> per position. At decode time, keys and values are derived from this latent on the fly. The cache size shrinks from H × d_k × L down to d<sub>c</sub> × L, where d_c ≪ H × d_k.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">MLA KV compression</div>
  c<sub>s</sub><sup>KV</sup> = W<sup>DKV</sup> h<sub>s</sub>  <span class="math-comment">  ← compress token h_s into latent (cached)</span><br>
  k<sub>s,i</sub><sup>A</sup> = W<sub>i</sub><sup>UK</sup> c<sub>s</sub><sup>KV</sup>  <span class="math-comment">  ← expand key for head i (at decode time)</span><br>
  v<sub>s,i</sub><sup>A</sup> = W<sub>i</sub><sup>UV</sup> c<sub>s</sub><sup>KV</sup>  <span class="math-comment">  ← expand value for head i (at decode time)</span>
</div>

<p>For DSA, this latent c<sub>s</sub><sup>KV</sup> is the natural unit for token selection. Rather than selecting per-head keys, DSA selects at the <em>latent level</em>: pick the top-k latent vectors c<sub>s</sub>, then expand to all heads. This is the MQA mode of MLA — one shared set of keys/values per selected token, queried by all heads simultaneously. This is critical for hardware efficiency: loading one latent and computing all heads together saturates memory bandwidth much better than loading per-head tensors.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">MLA architecture: MHA mode vs MQA mode</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to compare</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;margin-bottom:.875rem">
      <button class="k-btn active" id="mla-mha-btn" onclick="setMlaMode('mha')">MHA mode (training)</button>
      <button class="k-btn" id="mla-mqa-btn" onclick="setMlaMode('mqa')">MQA mode (DSA decoding)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="mla-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
    <p id="mla-desc" style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;line-height:1.6"></p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The key difference: in MHA mode, each head has its own W<sup>UK</sup> and W<sup>UV</sup> projection — independent keys/values per head. In MQA mode (used by DSA), a single shared latent c<sub>s</sub><sup>KV</sup> is used across all heads. This makes sparse selection tractable: instead of scoring L × H_A (key-value) tensors, DSA only needs to score L latent vectors.</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 LIGHTNING INDEXER -->
<section id="s-indexer" data-code="indexer">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Lightning Indexer</div>
<h2>Scoring 128K Tokens in Microseconds</h2>
<p>The central challenge: to select the top-k tokens, you first need to score all L tokens against the query. That sounds like it requires O(L) compute per query — which is fine — but the scores must be computed <em>before</em> running full attention. The lightning indexer is DeepSeek's answer: a lightweight attention mechanism that computes an index score I<sub>t,s</sub> for every (query, key) pair.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Lightning Indexer score</div>
  <span class="math-var">I</span><sub>t,s</sub> = Σ<sub>j=1..H<sup>I</sup></sub> w<sub>t,j</sub><sup>I</sup> · ReLU(q<sub>t,j</sub><sup>I</sup> · k<sub>s</sub><sup>I</sup>)
  <br><span class="math-comment">  where H^I = number of indexer heads (small), q^I and w^I derived from h_t, k^I derived from h_s</span>
</div>

<p>Three design choices make the indexer fast:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Few heads (H<sup>I</sup> ≪ H<sup>A</sup>)</strong> — the indexer has far fewer heads than the main attention, cutting FLOPs proportionally</li>
  <li><strong>ReLU activation</strong> — not softmax. Softmax requires a reduction across all L scores; ReLU is element-wise, enabling per-token streaming with no global sync</li>
  <li><strong>FP8 precision</strong> — the indexer runs in 8-bit floating point, halving memory bandwidth vs BF16 and enabling tensor-core utilization</li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">lightning indexer: how it scores tokens</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover over tokens</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="margin-bottom:.625rem;font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted2)">Query token: <strong style="color:var(--cyan)">"decorators"</strong> at position 100. Hover a token to see its index score. Top-k = 6 tokens are highlighted in green.</div>
    <canvas id="indexer-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block;cursor:pointer"></canvas>
    <div id="indexer-info" style="margin-top:.5rem;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);min-height:1.5em"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The indexer's purpose is not to be <em>perfectly accurate</em> — it just needs to produce a ranking that puts the truly important tokens in the top-k. The main attention mechanism handles the precise scoring. This separation of concerns is key: approximate fast scoring (indexer) + exact slow scoring (main attention on selected tokens).</p>

<h3>Weight w<sub>t,j</sub><sup>I</sup>: the query-dependent head importance</h3>
<p>Notice that the indexer formula includes a per-head scalar weight w<sub>t,j</sub><sup>I</sup> that varies per query token t. This lets the indexer learn to weight its own heads differently depending on what the query is asking about. For a query about Python syntax, the indexer might up-weight a "code structure" head; for a factual query, it might weight a "named entity" head more heavily.</p>
<p>The weight w<sub>t,j</sub><sup>I</sup> is derived from h<sub>t</sub> through a small learned projection — essentially a softmax over heads per query position. This adds expressiveness at negligible cost.</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 TOP-K SELECTION -->
<section id="s-topk" data-code="topk">
<div class="section-no">04 — Fine-Grained Token Selection</div>
<h2>From Scores to a Sparse Attention Window</h2>
<p>Given index scores I<sub>t,:</sub> for query token h<sub>t</sub>, DSA selects the top-k indices by score. Only those k latent vectors c<sub>s</sub> are loaded from the KV cache into SRAM for the main attention computation:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">DSA attention output</div>
  u<sub>t</sub> = Attn(h<sub>t</sub>, &#123;c<sub>s</sub> | I<sub>t,s</sub> ∈ Top-k(I<sub>t,:</sub>)&#125;)
  <br><span class="math-comment">  main attention runs on only k latent vectors, not all L</span>
</div>

<p>The interactive visualization below shows what the attention matrix looks like under different k values. Dense attention fills every cell. DSA leaves only k columns non-zero per row — the selected tokens differ per query position, creating an irregular sparse pattern (unlike sliding-window attention, which has a rigid local window).</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">attention sparsity pattern — dense vs DSA top-k</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click k values to compare</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="sparsity-ctrl">
      <label>k =</label>
      <div class="k-btns">
        <button class="k-btn active" data-k="all" onclick="setK('all')">Dense (all)</button>
        <button class="k-btn" data-k="8" onclick="setK('8')">8 (6%)</button>
        <button class="k-btn" data-k="4" onclick="setK('4')">4 (3%)</button>
        <button class="k-btn" data-k="2" onclick="setK('2')">2 (1.6%)</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="attn-wrap">
      <div class="attn-label" id="attn-label-top">query position →</div>
      <canvas id="attn-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block"></canvas>
      <div class="attn-label" id="attn-label-bot" style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--muted)">each row = one query; colored cells = selected KV tokens; black = skipped</div>
    </div>
    <div id="sparsity-stats" style="margin-top:.625rem;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted2)"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Notice that DSA's selection pattern is <em>content-adaptive</em> — different query tokens pick different sets of KV tokens based on their index scores. This is fundamentally different from local sliding-window attention (which forces a fixed window around each position) or strided attention (which picks every n-th token). DSA lets the model attend to semantically relevant tokens anywhere in the context.</p>

<h3>k = 2048 at 128K context: the production setting</h3>
<p>In DeepSeek-V3.2's actual deployment, k = 2048 with a context window of 128K tokens. That means each query attends to <strong>2048 / 131072 ≈ 1.6%</strong> of the context. The full-attention baseline attends to 100%. Yet benchmark results show no significant quality degradation — and on some long-context benchmarks, DSA slightly <em>outperforms</em> the dense baseline (4 points higher on AA-LCR3, better on Fiction.liveBench).</p>

<h3>Why does 1.6% work?</h3>
<p>Empirically, attention weights in long-context models are extremely concentrated. Studies show that in most attention heads, 1–5% of tokens account for 80%+ of the total attention weight. The long tail of near-zero attention weights represents tokens the model has already decided are irrelevant. DSA formalizes this empirical observation into an architectural constraint, then trains the model to be robust to it.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 TWO-STAGE TRAINING -->
<section id="s-training" data-code="training">
<div class="section-no">05 — Two-Stage Continued Training</div>
<h2>From Dense Checkpoint to Sparse Model</h2>
<p>DeepSeek-V3.2 did not train DSA from scratch. It started from DeepSeek-V3.1-Terminus (a fully trained 128K-context dense model) and applied <em>continued pre-training</em> in two stages. This is architecturally clean: the indexer is a new module that needs initialization, and the main model needs to adapt its weights to the sparse attention pattern.</p>

<div class="step-list" id="training-steps">
  <div class="step-box active">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Dense Warm-up Stage</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Keep full dense attention, freeze all model parameters, train only the lightning indexer. Objective: make the indexer's scores match the actual attention distribution (KL divergence loss). Learning rate: 10⁻³. Duration: 1000 steps × 16 sequences × 128K tokens = 2.1B tokens.</div>
      <div class="step-code">L_I = Σ_t KL(p_&#123;t,:&#125; ‖ softmax(I_&#123;t,:&#125;))</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Sparse Training Stage</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Activate top-k selection (k=2048). Unfreeze all parameters. Train main model on language modeling loss; train indexer on KL loss over only the selected token set. Learning rate: 7.3×10⁻⁶. Duration: 15,000 steps × 480 sequences × 128K = 943.7B tokens.</div>
      <div class="step-code">L_I = Σ_t KL(p_&#123;t,S_t&#125; ‖ softmax(I_&#123;t,S_t&#125;))  where S_t = top-k(I_&#123;t,:&#125;)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev" onclick="gotoStep(currentStep-1)">← Prev</button>
  <span id="step-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 2</span>
  <button id="step-next" onclick="gotoStep(currentStep+1)">Next →</button>
</div>

<h3>The detach trick: separate optimization paths</h3>
<p>A crucial implementation detail: during sparse training, the indexer's input is <em>detached from the computational graph</em>. The indexer sees gradients only from L<sub>I</sub> (the KL alignment loss), never from the language modeling loss. The main model is updated only by the LM loss, with no gradient flowing back through the indexer.</p>
<p>Why? If both losses shared gradients, the main model might learn to "game" the indexer — producing token representations that make the indexer's job easier but hurt the model's actual language modeling ability. Detaching prevents this coupling and keeps the two optimization problems clean.</p>

<h3>Short-context efficiency: masked MHA fallback</h3>
<p>For short sequences where prefilling is fast, DSA implements a special "masked MHA mode" that simulates sparse attention using standard dense operations. Below a context length threshold, dense attention with an appropriate mask is actually more efficient than the sparse indexer + selection pipeline (which has non-negligible kernel launch overhead). The model switches automatically between modes based on sequence length.</p>
</section>

<!-- §06 RESULTS -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Cost Reduction & Quality Parity</div>
<h2>What DSA Actually Buys</h2>
<p>The paper benchmarks inference cost on H800 clusters at a rental rate of $2/GPU-hour. The cost comparison is stark at long contexts:</p>

<table class="results-table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Context Length</th>
      <th>DeepSeek-V3.1 (Dense MLA)</th>
      <th>DeepSeek-V3.2 (DSA)</th>
      <th>Prefill Speedup</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>32K tokens</td>
      <td class="mn">~0.15$/M tokens</td>
